            if nxt is None:
                raise ParseError("unexpected end of file, wanted any of [TOKEN_L_BRACE]")
            else:
                raise ParseError(f"unexpected {token_to_rnix(nxt.typ)} at {nxt.start}..{nxt.end}, wanted any of [TOKEN_L_BRACE]", nxt)
        return parse_attrset(parser, is_rec=True, rec_tok=rec_tok)
    # Unexpected (e.g., keyword that should have been handled at higher levels? Like LET, IF, etc. as atom? Actually LET, IF, etc. are handled at higher levels (parse_let, parse_if, etc.), which are called before parse_atom (via parse_or->...->parse_app->parse_select->parse_atom). Wait parse_atom is lowest (highest precedence), called from parse_select, which is called from parse_app, etc., up to parse_lambda. So LET, IF, etc. should have been handled before reaching parse_atom (since parse_let checks for LET before delegating to parse_or). Good. So reaching parse_atom with LET means LET appears in atom position (e.g., as app arg without parens? Like f let...)? That should be error (since LET cannot be atom without parens). Good. So report error.
